# reading 2 of hume-dialogues-1.md by claude-opus-5-5
atom	abstract_invincible	reason, considered in an abstract view, furnishes invincible arguments against itself
atom	conviction	we retain conviction or assurance on some subject
atom	wide	our arguments lose their advantage and run wide of common life
atom	counter	sceptical reasonings oppose and counterbalance our arguments
atom	equal_weight	the one has no more weight than the other
atom	suspense	the mind remains in suspense between them
atom	triumph	scepticism triumphs
# S1 reports the sceptics' view: we keep conviction only because sceptical reasonings cannot counterpoise the arguments from sense and experience.
P1	S1	abstract_invincible ∧ (counter → ¬conviction)	says sceptics
P2	S2	wide → counter
# S3 is read as following from the counterbalancing in S2: the two then have equal weight.
P3	S3	counter → equal_weight
# "must remain in suspense" is read as a conditional consequence of the equal weight, not as a modal claim.
P4	S4	equal_weight → suspense
P5	S4	suspense → triumph	kind conceptual
C1	S4	wide → suspense	from P2 P3 P4
C	S4	wide → triumph	from C1 P5
